  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/f92cef7097e146629d85d63316c7c32f62ab3f1062eae012714a737c5c388f7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=(x+3)(x+1{)}^{2}(x-1)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=-3,1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=-3,1$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{I}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{I}.$",
